<section class="page-hero">
  <div id="ambient-canvas" class="ambient-canvas"></div>
  <div class="container page-hero-inner">
    <span class="eyebrow">Free Tool</span>
    <h1>Free Sitemap Finder — Find Any Website's XML Sitemap in Seconds</h1>
    <p>Enter a domain and this <strong>sitemap finder</strong> lists every common place a sitemap lives, with one-click links to check each, plus Google searches that surface it. Paste the site's robots.txt and it pulls out and checks every <code>Sitemap:</code> line. It never contacts the site itself, so it works without a login and nothing leaves your browser.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="container contact-grid llms-tool-grid">
    <div>
      <form class="contact-form llms-form" id="sf-form" onsubmit="return false;" aria-label="Sitemap finder form">
        <div>
          <label for="sf-domain">Website or domain</label>
          <input type="text" id="sf-domain" placeholder="e.g. example.com or https://www.example.com">
        </div>
        <div>
          <label for="sf-robots">Paste the site's robots.txt (optional)</label>
          <textarea id="sf-robots" rows="7" placeholder="User-agent: *&#10;Disallow: /admin/&#10;Sitemap: https://example.com/sitemap_index.xml"></textarea>
          <small style="display:block;margin-top:6px;color:#6b6459;">Open the robots.txt link on the right, copy what you see and paste it here to extract and check the Sitemap lines.</small>
        </div>
      </form>
    </div>

    <div class="contact-info-card llms-preview-card">
      <div class="llms-preview-head">
        <strong>Where to look</strong>
        <span id="sf-status" class="llms-status">Waiting for input</span>
      </div>
      <div id="sf-output" style="max-height:520px;overflow:auto;font-size:0.95rem;line-height:1.7;">Enter a domain to start.</div>
      <div class="llms-actions" style="margin-top:14px;display:flex;gap:10px;flex-wrap:wrap;">
        <button type="button" class="btn btn-primary" id="sf-copy">Copy the list</button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="container llms-explainer">

    <h2>How to Find a Website's Sitemap</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Open the robots.txt first.</strong> Add <code>/robots.txt</code> to the domain. A <code>Sitemap:</code> line there is the site's own statement of where its sitemap is.</li>
      <li><strong>Try the common paths.</strong> <code>/sitemap.xml</code> is the most common, followed by <code>/sitemap_index.xml</code> (used by Yoast SEO and Rank Math), <code>/wp-sitemap.xml</code> (WordPress core) and <code>/sitemap-index.xml</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>Search Google.</strong> The links on the right run <code>site:</code> searches that often reveal a sitemap file the common paths miss.</li>
      <li><strong>Check your own property.</strong> If it is your site, open Sitemaps in Google Search Console to see what has been submitted.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>What We Found on 66 Real Sites</h2>
    <p>We checked 66 business websites on 20 October 2026 with the same method this tool uses: the robots.txt file and five common sitemap paths. Sites are not named.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>47 of 66 (71%) had a working sitemap</strong> at one of those places. For the other 19 we found none by these methods.</li>
      <li><strong>44 of 66 answered at <code>/sitemap.xml</code>,</strong> after redirects. Of the 47 sites that had a sitemap, only 3 had one that was not reachable at <code>/sitemap.xml</code>, so that one path finds about 94% of them.</li>
      <li><strong>39 robots.txt files declared a sitemap,</strong> and 35 of those lines pointed at a working file. The other 4 pointed at a URL that did not return a valid sitemap.</li>
      <li><strong>11 sites had a working sitemap that robots.txt never mentioned,</strong> so checking only the robots.txt would have missed them.</li>
      <li><strong>23 sites answered at more than one path,</strong> often because a WordPress site serves its core sitemap and a plugin's sitemap together or redirects one to the other.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The practical rule is to check both: the robots.txt for what the site declares, and the common paths for what is actually there. Our guide to the <a href="/blog/robots-txt-sitemap">robots.txt sitemap field</a> explains the declaration, and our look at <a href="/blog/yoast-seo-sitemap">what real Yoast sitemaps contain</a> explains the <code>/sitemap_index.xml</code> pattern.</p>

    <h2>What to Do Once You Find the Sitemap</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Check it is valid.</strong> Paste it into the <a href="/tools/sitemap-validator">sitemap validator</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>List the URLs.</strong> Use the <a href="/tools/sitemap-url-extractor">sitemap URL extractor</a> to turn it into a clean list.</li>
      <li><strong>Build a visitor page.</strong> The <a href="/tools/html-sitemap-generator">HTML sitemap generator</a> turns the URL list into a page for readers.</li>
      <li><strong>Make one if there is none.</strong> The <a href="/tools/sitemap-generator">XML sitemap generator</a> creates a file from a list of URLs, and <a href="/blog/how-to-create-a-sitemap">how to create a sitemap</a> explains the rules.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Sitemap Finder FAQs</h2>
    <div class="faq-list" style="margin-top: 22px;">
      <details>
        <summary>Where is a website's sitemap usually located?<span class="faq-icon"></span></summary>
        <p>Most often at <code>/sitemap.xml</code>. WordPress sites with Yoast SEO or Rank Math usually use <code>/sitemap_index.xml</code>, and WordPress itself serves <code>/wp-sitemap.xml</code>. The robots.txt file may also list it.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Does this tool fetch the site's sitemap for me?<span class="faq-icon"></span></summary>
        <p>No. Browsers block one site from reading another's files, so the tool lists where to look and gives you links to open. You can paste a robots.txt to have its Sitemap lines extracted and checked.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>What if no sitemap is found?<span class="faq-icon"></span></summary>
        <p>The site may not have one, or it may sit at an unusual address. Try the Google searches in the tool. Not having a sitemap does not stop a site being crawled, but it helps search engines find pages.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Why does my robots.txt Sitemap line need a full URL?<span class="faq-icon"></span></summary>
        <p>The Sitemap line should give the full, absolute URL, including https and the domain. A relative path is not valid.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Can a site have more than one sitemap?<span class="faq-icon"></span></summary>
        <p>Yes. Large sites use a sitemap index that lists several sitemap files, and a single sitemap is limited to 50MB uncompressed or 50,000 URLs.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Does this tool store or send my data anywhere?<span class="faq-icon"></span></summary>
        <p>No. Everything is built in your browser with JavaScript — nothing is uploaded, logged, or stored on any server, including ours.</p>
      </details>
    </div>

    <div class="cta-band" style="margin-top: 44px;">
      <span class="cta-orb cta-orb-1"></span>
      <span class="cta-orb cta-orb-2"></span>
      <h2>Need a technical SEO check?</h2>
      <p>Contomatix does audits, on-page work and link building for businesses and agencies.</p>
      <div class="cta-actions">
        <a href="/contact" class="btn btn-primary">Get in touch</a>
        <a href="https://wa.me/<%= site.whatsappNumber %>" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="btn btn-glass">Chat on WhatsApp</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

